# Go core conventions

Source of truth: [Effective Go](https://go.dev/doc/effective_go),
[Go Doc Comments](https://go.dev/doc/comment). When this rule and a project's own
conventions disagree, follow the project and say so.

## Formatting

- `gofmt` output is the only correct formatting. Never hand-format; never argue with it.
  Run `gofmt -l .` and treat any output as a defect.
- Tabs indent, spaces align. There is no line-length limit; break long lines with an
  extra tab of indentation rather than inventing a wrap column.
- Since Go 1.19 `gofmt` also reformats doc comments, so let it own list, heading and
  code-block layout inside comments too.

## Naming

- Package names: lower case, single word, no underscores, no mixedCaps. The package
  name is the last element of the import path (`encoding/base64` → `base64`).
- Never name a package `util`, `common`, `misc`, `helpers`, `types`, or `api` — those
  "provide clients with no sense of what the package contains".
- Avoid stutter. From package `http`, the type is `Server`, not `HTTPServer`;
  the constructor is `list.New()`, not `list.NewList()`.
- Use `MixedCaps` / `mixedCaps`, never `snake_case`, for multiword names.
- One-method interfaces take the method name plus `-er`: `Reader`, `Writer`,
  `Formatter`. Do not reuse canonical method names (`Read`, `Write`, `Close`,
  `String`, `Len`) unless the signature and meaning match.
- Getters drop `Get`: field `owner` → method `Owner()`, setter `SetOwner()`.
- Keep the receiver name identical across every method of a type.
- Sentinel errors are `ErrSomething`; error types are `SomethingError`.

## Doc comments

- Every exported identifier gets a doc comment, immediately above the declaration
  with no blank line between.
- Write complete sentences that begin with the name being documented:
  `// Quote returns a double-quoted Go string literal ...`, `// A Reader serves ...`,
  `// Package tar implements ...`.
- Boolean-returning functions say "reports whether", never "returns true if ... or not".
- Deprecate with a paragraph starting `Deprecated: ` and pointing at the replacement.
- Link to other symbols with doc links: `[Name]`, `[pkg.Name]`, `[*bytes.Buffer]`.
- Headings are `# Heading` (hash, space, blank line before and after, unindented).

## Errors

- Return errors; do not panic across a package boundary. `panic` is for
  impossible states, and `recover` only ever runs inside a deferred function.
- Never discard an error with `_`. Handle it, wrap it, or explain in a comment why
  dropping it is correct.
- Prefix error strings with their origin: `"image: unknown format"`.
- Wrap with `%w` **only** when the wrapped error is part of your API contract:
  "Wrap an error to expose it to callers. Do not wrap an error when doing so would
  expose implementation details." To hide the cause, format with `%v`.
- Inspect with `errors.Is` and `errors.As`, never with `==` on a wrapped error and
  never by string matching.
- Return `fmt.Errorf("...: %w", ErrNotFound)` rather than the bare sentinel so callers
  get context and `errors.Is` still works.

## Control flow and structure

- Handle the error case first and return early; do not write `else` after a `return`.
- Prefer `for i := range n` / `for i, v := range s` over manual index arithmetic.
- Do not build abstractions before there are two concrete users of them.
- Reach for a type parameter only after you have written the same code for different
  types more than once. If all you need is to call a method, use an interface,
  not a type parameter.

## Concurrency

- "Do not communicate by sharing memory; instead, share memory by communicating."
- Every goroutine needs a defined exit path. If you cannot say what stops it,
  it is a leak.
- Pass `context.Context` as the first parameter (`ctx`) on any call path that can
  block, do I/O, or cross a process boundary. Never store one in a struct field.
- Bound parallelism with a fixed worker count; do not spawn one goroutine per item
  of unbounded input.
- Maps are not safe for concurrent write; guard with `sync.RWMutex` or redesign.
- Run tests for concurrent code with `-race`.
